    "parts": [
      {
        "content": "## Summary\n\n**Target fact:** `F:ml430-nat-modeq-refl-d870c8f5` — Nat.ModEq.refl  \n**Formal statement:** `∀ {n : ℕ} (a : ℕ), a ≡ a [MOD n]`\n\n**Structural shape:** This is a reflexivity property for the modular equivalence relation. It states that any natural number is congruent to itself modulo any modulus. The goal has a universal quantifier over the modulus `n` (implicit) and the value `a`, concluding with a reflexive modular equivalence `a ≡ a [MOD n]`.\n\n**Dependencies:** None. This fact has no dependencies and unlocks nothing.\n\n**Available premises:** The nat prelude contains `Nat.mod_eq_refl : ∀ (x0 x1 : AxNat) → AxNat.modEq x0 x1 x1`, which is exactly the kernel theorem for this reflexivity property (with arguments in a slightly different order).\n\n**Similar eligible facts:** The frontier contains several other modular equivalence facts in the Nat fragment:\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-symm-0a3d4d18` (symmetry)\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-trans-ef9d1c46` (transitivity)  \n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-one-516d46e8` (equivalence mod 1)\n- `F:ml430-nat-modeq-add-left-e83f0700`, `F:ml430-nat-modeq-add-right-8e2ca0cc` (congruence properties)\n- Several other modeq facts with similar structure\n\n**Operations:** No registered operations currently target this fact.",
        "id": null,
        "provider_name": null,
        "provider_details": null,
        "part_kind": "text"
      }
    ],
